WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.144 --> 00:00:04.187
- What's up, believe nation?

00:00:04.187 --> 00:00:07.139
It's Evan, I'm excited
to launch a new series.

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:09.522
We're code-naming it Believe Love.

00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:12.444
The idea here is, it can be really hard

00:00:12.444 --> 00:00:14.284
for entrepreneurs, not
just to start a business,

00:00:14.284 --> 00:00:17.077
you know how crazy it is to
start and run a business,

00:00:17.077 --> 00:00:19.689
but also to manage your
relationship at the same time.

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:22.143
Maybe the person you're with
is also an entrepreneur,

00:00:22.143 --> 00:00:24.934
and you're fighting to even
spend any kind of time together,

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:26.361
'cause you're both so busy.

00:00:26.361 --> 00:00:28.566
Maybe the person you're
with is not an entrepreneur,

00:00:28.566 --> 00:00:29.984
and has a nine-to-five job,

00:00:29.984 --> 00:00:31.982
and that can be a struggle, just in terms

00:00:31.982 --> 00:00:34.437
of understanding each other,
and support that you need.

00:00:34.437 --> 00:00:37.705
And so, I get so many
questions from entrepreneurs,

00:00:37.705 --> 00:00:40.416
and wannabe entrepreneurs,
that deal with relationships,

00:00:40.416 --> 00:00:42.783
and I thought: how about
we make it a weekly series.

00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:45.171
It's going to be a test;
we'll see what you guys think.

00:00:45.171 --> 00:00:47.177
To showcase different pieces of advice

00:00:47.177 --> 00:00:48.581
that you might be able to use,

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.552
bring it to your life, and hopefully

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:52.155
help you have healthier, strongier,

00:00:52.155 --> 00:00:55.158
strongier, strongier,
(laughing)

00:00:55.158 --> 00:00:57.619
happier relationships,
because when you're happy

00:00:57.619 --> 00:00:59.449
at home too, you have so much more energy

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:00.677
that you can bring to your business,

00:01:00.677 --> 00:01:02.310
and go off and do amazing things.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.061
And so today, we're going
to learn from the legendary

00:01:05.061 --> 00:01:07.348
Iyanla Vanzant, and her tips

00:01:07.348 --> 00:01:10.109
on how to fight fairly.

00:01:10.109 --> 00:01:12.731
- How to resolve differences,

00:01:12.731 --> 00:01:14.810
and upsets, in a relationship.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.642
So, I'm going to shorten that

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:18.649
and make it: how to fight fair.

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:21.343
Fights, meaning upsets, disagreements,

00:01:21.343 --> 00:01:23.868
and arguments, are normal,

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:26.667
even in the best of relationships.

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:29.112
What matters is, how you handle it.

00:01:29.112 --> 00:01:31.034
Now, I know there are those people

00:01:31.034 --> 00:01:34.071
who think the first time
they have a disagreement

00:01:34.071 --> 00:01:37.989
or an argument, that the
relationship is over.

00:01:37.989 --> 00:01:39.461
Wrong!

00:01:39.461 --> 00:01:42.883
What makes an argument, or an upset,

00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:45.352
or a disagreement, even a slight breakdown

00:01:45.352 --> 00:01:47.649
in a relationship, meaningful,

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:50.932
is how you handle it,

00:01:50.932 --> 00:01:52.985
and how you handle yourself.

00:01:52.985 --> 00:01:55.566
So, whether it's a
long-standing love ship,

00:01:55.566 --> 00:01:57.966
or a new relationship, understanding

00:01:57.966 --> 00:02:01.708
how to handle those difficult
places, those upsets,

00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:04.891
and fighting fairly, is crucial

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:08.473
for the success of any
and every relationship.

00:02:08.473 --> 00:02:10.849
Normally, when folks have a disagreement

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:13.495
or an argument, even if
they love each other,

00:02:13.495 --> 00:02:17.972
either of them instinctively wants to win

00:02:17.972 --> 00:02:19.534
and prove that they're right.

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:22.703
However, when you are in a relationship,

00:02:22.703 --> 00:02:25.386
you really have to understand

00:02:25.386 --> 00:02:29.446
that fighting to be right,
holding on to your position,

00:02:29.446 --> 00:02:33.432
is usually not going to
turn out well for you.

00:02:33.432 --> 00:02:37.096
In most arguments, we expect
there will be a winner

00:02:37.096 --> 00:02:39.356
and a loser; when, however,

00:02:39.356 --> 00:02:42.082
we are dealing with a loving relationship,

00:02:42.082 --> 00:02:45.375
an argument can either
make both of you winners,

00:02:45.375 --> 00:02:48.063
or make both of you suffer,

00:02:48.063 --> 00:02:50.269
depending on how you do it.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:53.647
So, here's a few tips, few
things that I've learned

00:02:53.647 --> 00:02:55.835
along the way, that I'd
like to share with you,

00:02:55.835 --> 00:02:58.344
because I was a gotta-win person.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.043
First, stay focused

00:03:01.043 --> 00:03:03.705
on the current conflict,

00:03:03.705 --> 00:03:06.926
and resist the temptation
to be a history major.

00:03:06.926 --> 00:03:08.961
I'll explain that in a little bit,

00:03:08.961 --> 00:03:11.446
but when things get heated, it is easy

00:03:11.446 --> 00:03:13.492
to lose sight of the real conflict,

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:16.035
especially when it feels
like you are the one

00:03:16.035 --> 00:03:17.698
that's losing the argument.

00:03:17.698 --> 00:03:19.906
When that happens, you will be tempted,

00:03:19.906 --> 00:03:22.177
particularly us ladies,

00:03:22.177 --> 00:03:24.834
to bring up everything,

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:26.420
all of the history,

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.471
of everything your partner
or friend has done wrong.

00:03:29.471 --> 00:03:32.837
You may think that you're using evidence

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:35.082
to make your point stronger,

00:03:35.082 --> 00:03:38.827
however, what happens
is you are contributing

00:03:38.827 --> 00:03:41.436
to a shut-down, and frustration

00:03:41.436 --> 00:03:45.205
that will not resolve the
issue or end the argument.

00:03:45.205 --> 00:03:47.800
So stick to what the upset is about,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.893
what angered you in this moment,

00:03:49.893 --> 00:03:52.323
rather than every other upset

00:03:52.323 --> 00:03:55.288
that you never brought to the table.

00:03:55.288 --> 00:03:58.273
It will serve you to focus on one aspect,

00:03:58.273 --> 00:04:00.237
and solve that one issue,

00:04:00.237 --> 00:04:02.953
instead of going all over the place

00:04:02.953 --> 00:04:06.742
and dragging the other person
through the mud of the past.

00:04:06.742 --> 00:04:10.435
Next, and this is really,
really, really important:

00:04:10.435 --> 00:04:12.952
don't low-blow the other person.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:15.049
There are times in a relationship

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:17.119
when all you see is red, particularly

00:04:17.119 --> 00:04:18.592
when you're arguing.

00:04:18.592 --> 00:04:20.123
When this happens,

00:04:20.123 --> 00:04:23.230
the easiest way to hurt your partner,

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.174
or the person you're in upset with,

00:04:25.174 --> 00:04:27.368
is to hit them where it hurts,

00:04:27.368 --> 00:04:29.066
by calling them names,

00:04:29.066 --> 00:04:31.528
or talking about how much
weight they've gained,

00:04:31.528 --> 00:04:34.171
or how many things
they've failed at doing.

00:04:34.171 --> 00:04:36.997
Not only are you pulling
on, and triggering

00:04:36.997 --> 00:04:39.657
a sensitive nerve, digging around

00:04:39.657 --> 00:04:44.116
an insecurity, you are
not fighting fairly.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:47.221
Oh, you may manage to bring
the other person to tears,

00:04:47.221 --> 00:04:49.388
to make them feel weak,

00:04:49.388 --> 00:04:51.504
but why would you ever want to do that?

00:04:51.504 --> 00:04:53.922
Just to prove that you're
right and they're wrong?

00:04:53.922 --> 00:04:56.161
If you want to fight fairly,

00:04:56.161 --> 00:04:58.421
don't blow the other person,

00:04:58.421 --> 00:05:00.538
because that traumatizes them,

00:05:00.538 --> 00:05:02.730
and it creates something that you

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:06.701
will only have to clean up later on, okay?

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:07.784
Number three:

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.716
resist the temptation to be sarcastic,

00:05:12.716 --> 00:05:14.697
because sarcasm hurts.

00:05:14.697 --> 00:05:16.080
Oh, really?

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.453
Who, you?

00:05:17.453 --> 00:05:19.276
Yeah, I bet you will.

00:05:19.276 --> 00:05:21.978
These are just a few of the biting,

00:05:21.978 --> 00:05:24.180
stinging, sarcastic remarks

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.789
that not only hurt, but also
piss the other person off,

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:30.702
and force them to start low-blowing you,

00:05:30.702 --> 00:05:32.526
or bringing up past issues,

00:05:32.526 --> 00:05:34.853
just to get even.

00:05:34.853 --> 00:05:37.806
And when that gets started,

00:05:37.806 --> 00:05:39.977
a misunderstanding can lead

00:05:39.977 --> 00:05:42.778
to an all-out terroristic war.

00:05:42.778 --> 00:05:44.159
And guess what?

00:05:44.159 --> 00:05:47.908
Wars create casualties and fatalities.

00:05:47.908 --> 00:05:51.348
Sarcasm not only wounds the ego,

00:05:51.348 --> 00:05:53.092
it damages the heart

00:05:53.092 --> 00:05:55.678
and it diminishes the other person,

00:05:55.678 --> 00:05:57.670
while it dishonors you.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.087
Stay away from the low blows.

00:06:01.496 --> 00:06:02.846
Number four:

00:06:02.846 --> 00:06:05.151
don't come into an argument

00:06:05.151 --> 00:06:07.472
with your mind made up

00:06:07.472 --> 00:06:10.060
that the other person is
wrong and you are right.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:11.807
(laughing)
Okay?

00:06:11.807 --> 00:06:15.947
Be willing to ask questions
and share your assumptions.

00:06:15.947 --> 00:06:18.494
Be willing to hear what is said,

00:06:18.494 --> 00:06:22.737
and without relying on
what you have already

00:06:22.737 --> 00:06:24.755
made up, in your mind,

00:06:24.755 --> 00:06:27.282
when the other person wasn't even present.

00:06:27.282 --> 00:06:30.071
Talk about what you
feel and what you felt.

00:06:30.071 --> 00:06:31.971
Talk about your perspective

00:06:31.971 --> 00:06:34.041
and what you think is going on,

00:06:34.041 --> 00:06:36.830
without making accusations
and assumptions.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:40.878
Be willing to get off your position

00:06:40.878 --> 00:06:42.767
in response to what you hear,

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:45.106
because as long as you stay planted

00:06:45.106 --> 00:06:46.593
in the need to be right,

00:06:46.593 --> 00:06:50.483
the argument or the upset
will linger and grow.

00:06:50.483 --> 00:06:53.012
Stay open to the possibility

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:55.261
that you may have been mistaken,

00:06:55.261 --> 00:06:58.494
even in that conversation
you had in your own head,

00:06:58.494 --> 00:07:00.308
where you won.

00:07:00.308 --> 00:07:01.556
Number five:

00:07:01.556 --> 00:07:03.598
supporting that last point,

00:07:03.598 --> 00:07:06.241
you must listen to each other.

00:07:06.241 --> 00:07:07.455
I know it's hard,

00:07:07.455 --> 00:07:10.267
but it is an absolute must.

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:12.369
In a healthy relationship,

00:07:12.369 --> 00:07:14.900
you must respect each other's opinion,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.834
and you cannot do that if
you're not willing to listen.

00:07:18.834 --> 00:07:19.930
And guess what?

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.266
The best way to be heard

00:07:22.266 --> 00:07:25.001
is to be willing to hear somebody else.

00:07:25.001 --> 00:07:27.649
Now, in listening, you need not agree,

00:07:27.649 --> 00:07:30.545
but you must listen to what is being said.

00:07:30.545 --> 00:07:33.942
You may feel attacked or
wronged by an accusation,

00:07:33.942 --> 00:07:37.661
or maybe a very invalid point, however,

00:07:37.661 --> 00:07:40.504
it is important to fair fighting

00:07:40.504 --> 00:07:44.116
to make an effort not
to cut the person off,

00:07:44.116 --> 00:07:45.904
and to listen to what they're saying.

00:07:45.904 --> 00:07:47.288
And while you're listening,

00:07:47.288 --> 00:07:50.502
don't formulate your response in your head

00:07:50.502 --> 00:07:53.575
and then, respond to what you've heard

00:07:53.575 --> 00:07:56.748
rather than what was actually said.

00:07:56.748 --> 00:07:59.601
In spite of what most people think,

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:02.655
an argument can help a
relationship get better,

00:08:02.655 --> 00:08:06.002
and help both of you
understand each other more.

00:08:06.002 --> 00:08:08.638
However, if you do not listen

00:08:08.638 --> 00:08:11.336
you cannot hear what is being said.

00:08:11.336 --> 00:08:13.202
You will make stuff up

00:08:13.202 --> 00:08:16.126
that has nothing to do
with the other person,

00:08:16.126 --> 00:08:17.658
or what is going on.

00:08:17.658 --> 00:08:20.128
Remember, you need not agree,

00:08:20.128 --> 00:08:23.246
but you want to make the effort to listen

00:08:23.246 --> 00:08:26.767
and hear, so that you will be heard.

00:08:26.767 --> 00:08:29.409
Number six: mmmm,

00:08:29.409 --> 00:08:30.635
keep breathing.

00:08:30.635 --> 00:08:33.154
And I know this sounds stupid,

00:08:33.154 --> 00:08:35.633
but against everything,

00:08:35.633 --> 00:08:37.583
I want you to do your best

00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:40.181
not to raise your voice.

00:08:40.181 --> 00:08:42.588
I know, I know.
(laughing)

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:44.524
That sounds crazy in an argument.

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:47.008
But all arguments don't have to operate

00:08:47.008 --> 00:08:49.751
at a 10; you can do it at a five,

00:08:49.751 --> 00:08:52.001
because when you raise your voice,

00:08:52.001 --> 00:08:55.444
it will no only infuriate
the other person,

00:08:55.444 --> 00:08:57.997
who will probably start yelling back,

00:08:57.997 --> 00:08:59.269
it may frighten them,

00:08:59.269 --> 00:09:02.182
making them even more defensive.

00:09:02.182 --> 00:09:03.723
And when they're defensive,

00:09:03.723 --> 00:09:06.130
they cannot be available to hear you;

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.953
they can't be available to a resolution.

00:09:08.953 --> 00:09:12.957
Raised voices can make a
mountain out of a molehill,

00:09:12.957 --> 00:09:14.946
and cloud the issue.

00:09:14.946 --> 00:09:17.362
Now, you're not talking to a wild child

00:09:17.362 --> 00:09:18.455
or a stranger;

00:09:18.455 --> 00:09:20.940
not sure why you would yell
at either of them either,

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.225
but, I do know that yelling

00:09:24.225 --> 00:09:27.803
does not make your point
stronger or more right.

00:09:27.803 --> 00:09:30.212
Staying in control of yourself,

00:09:30.212 --> 00:09:32.333
speaking with a calm voice,

00:09:32.333 --> 00:09:35.212
even if you go from three to five to six,

00:09:35.212 --> 00:09:36.783
hold it right there.

00:09:36.783 --> 00:09:40.691
A calm voice can also
calm the other person down

00:09:40.691 --> 00:09:44.133
and turn an argument into a discussion,

00:09:44.133 --> 00:09:46.631
rather than a fight.

00:09:46.631 --> 00:09:48.726
Here's number seven:

00:09:48.726 --> 00:09:50.895
to fight fairly,

00:09:50.895 --> 00:09:53.298
you must be willing to tell the truth.

00:09:53.298 --> 00:09:56.801
Withholding information,
distorting information,

00:09:56.801 --> 00:09:59.001
sugar-coating bad behavior,

00:09:59.001 --> 00:10:01.584
or out-and-out being dishonest

00:10:01.584 --> 00:10:04.383
is going to backfire sooner or later.

00:10:04.383 --> 00:10:08.050
Lying will make the
other person suspicious.

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.389
People know when you're
not telling the truth,

00:10:10.389 --> 00:10:12.752
and that destroys trust.

00:10:12.752 --> 00:10:15.394
Tell the truth, even when it's hard.

00:10:15.394 --> 00:10:17.462
Speak your mind and your truth

00:10:17.462 --> 00:10:21.507
in a way that doesn't
intentionally inflict harm.

00:10:21.507 --> 00:10:23.598
Say what needs to be said,

00:10:23.598 --> 00:10:26.107
and be willing to hear the truth

00:10:26.107 --> 00:10:28.613
about you and your behavior.

00:10:28.613 --> 00:10:31.780
Number eight, this is another big one:

00:10:33.316 --> 00:10:36.274
don't blame your failures

00:10:36.274 --> 00:10:39.085
and shortcomings, or behaviors,

00:10:39.085 --> 00:10:40.658
on anyone else.

00:10:40.658 --> 00:10:42.347
In the midst of an argument,

00:10:42.347 --> 00:10:45.664
it is absolutely essential that you own

00:10:45.664 --> 00:10:49.321
and accept responsibility
for your behavior,

00:10:49.321 --> 00:10:53.612
your choices, and the
consequences of both of those.

00:10:53.612 --> 00:10:56.028
Nothing takes a disagreement

00:10:56.028 --> 00:10:59.529
down the road to disaster faster

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:01.746
than when you are blaming the other person

00:11:01.746 --> 00:11:03.894
for what you did or did not do.

00:11:03.894 --> 00:11:06.978
Don't blame anyone else
for your misfortunes,

00:11:06.978 --> 00:11:09.089
your failures, or your shortcomings.

00:11:09.089 --> 00:11:12.543
If you feel strongly about
something someone did,

00:11:12.543 --> 00:11:15.840
speak about it, owning your part in it.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:18.289
It may not be anybody's fault.

00:11:18.289 --> 00:11:20.532
Sometimes stuff just happens.

00:11:20.532 --> 00:11:23.759
Sometimes it could be
a big misunderstanding,

00:11:23.759 --> 00:11:25.421
but you've got to tell the truth

00:11:25.421 --> 00:11:27.403
about what you did, what you didn't do,

00:11:27.403 --> 00:11:29.663
what you feel, what you said,

00:11:29.663 --> 00:11:33.025
what you heard, and
what you want right now.

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:35.409
It may take some effort and humility

00:11:35.409 --> 00:11:38.830
to put your arms around
someone after a fresh fight;

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.291
this is why it is so important

00:11:41.291 --> 00:11:44.035
to fight in a way that shows

00:11:44.035 --> 00:11:46.147
that both of you respect each other,

00:11:46.147 --> 00:11:47.971
and that you love each other.

00:11:47.971 --> 00:11:51.376
Refuse to allow big or small

00:11:51.376 --> 00:11:55.683
irritating misunderstandings
to come in the way

00:11:55.683 --> 00:11:57.404
of the love that you share.

00:11:57.404 --> 00:11:58.675
Fight fairly.

00:11:58.675 --> 00:12:00.916
Stay engaged.

00:12:00.916 --> 00:12:04.176
Fighting fairly may not
only help you understand

00:12:04.176 --> 00:12:06.046
each other better, but it means

00:12:06.046 --> 00:12:09.285
that you will have a
healthier relationship

00:12:09.285 --> 00:12:10.732
when the dust settles.

00:12:10.732 --> 00:12:14.152
Remember, fighting need

00:12:14.152 --> 00:12:16.827
not be fatal.

00:12:16.827 --> 00:12:18.774
Pick your battles wisely,

00:12:18.774 --> 00:12:20.983
and always fight

00:12:20.983 --> 00:12:23.665
without drawing blood.

00:12:23.665 --> 00:12:24.935
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:24.935 --> 00:12:26.012
I hope you enjoyed.

00:12:26.012 --> 00:12:28.019
I'd love to know: what did
you think of this video?

00:12:28.019 --> 00:12:29.627
Did you like some of the points here?

00:12:29.627 --> 00:12:32.306
Which one is most
relevant, applicable to you

00:12:32.306 --> 00:12:33.666
in your situation right now?

